<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giuseppe Mazzini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giuseppe Mazzini was a Genoese revolutionary and founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a>, who campaigned for a united, republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1848 he joined the republicans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and in 1849 he led the triumvirate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> until French troops restored papal rule. He spent most of his life in exile, and Italy was united as a monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Mazzini" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Mazzini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the partitions, Polish poets and musicians kept alive a national culture without a state, and <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b>, who founded Young Italy in 1831, preached the brotherhood of free nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b> revived the republican idea in Italy, Brazil’s radical liberals founded a Republican Party in 1870, and Spain had two republics, in 1873–1874 and 1931–1939.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Moderates led by Casati favoured union with Piedmont, while republicans such as Cattaneo and <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b> wanted to delay the question.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-milan">Provisional Government of Milan</a></li><li><blockquote>A triumvirate of <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b>, Aurelio Saffi and Carlo Armellini governed.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-republic-1849">Roman Republic (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giuseppe Mazzini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
